{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b", "content": "**A Whirlwind Week in Japan**\n\nLanding in Tokyo felt like stepping into a living postcard—neon signs flickered above bustling streets while the scent of yakitori drifted from tiny izakayas. My first morning began at the Meiji Shrine, where towering cedars whispered centuries of tradition. After a quiet prayer, I dove into Harajuku’s quirky fashion scene, snapping photos of cosplay enthusiasts and sipping matcha‑latte art that looked too beautiful to drink.\n\nDay two took me on the Shinkansen to Kyoto, the bullet train gliding silently past rice paddies and distant mountains. In the ancient capital, I wandered the Philosopher’s Path, lined with cherry‑blossom trees that, even out of season, cast a delicate pink hue over the canal. The golden glow of Kinkaku‑ji reflected perfectly in its pond, and I spent an hour simply watching koi glide beneath the surface.\n\nNo trip to Japan is complete without a tea ceremony. In a modest machiya townhouse, a kimono‑clad host guided me through the precise movements—whisking powdered matcha, feeling the warmth of the bowl, and savoring the bitter‑sweet foam. The ritual felt like a meditation, a reminder to slow down amid the city’s rush.\n\nEvenings were reserved for food adventures: slurping steaming bowls of ramen in Osaka’s Dotonbori district, trying takoyaki from a street stall, and ending each night with a quiet stroll along the river, watching lanterns dance on the water.\n\nJapan’s seamless blend of ultra‑modern efficiency and deep‑rooted serenity left me recharged and inspired. I boarded the plane home with a suitcase full of souvenirs, a heart full of memories, and a promise to return—perhaps next time to explore the snow‑capped peaks of Hokkaido or the tranquil temples of Shikoku. Until then, the echo of temple bells and the taste of fresh sushi will keep my wanderlust alive.", "logprobs": [{"toke